* {{rhymes|en|ɔːt|s=1}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/fɹɔt/|/fɹɑt/}}
* {{homophones|en|frot}} {{qualifier|in accents with the [[cot-caught merger]]}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eyḱ-}}
From {{der|en|enm|fraght}}, {{m|enm|freght}}, from {{der|en|dum|vracht}} or {{der|en|gml|vracht||freight money}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gem-pro|*fra-|pos=intensive prefix}} + {{der|en|gem-pro|*aihtiz||possession}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂eyḱ-||to possess}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|frēht||earnings}}, {{cog|ang|ǣht||sc=Latinx|owndom}}, and a {{doublet|en|freight|nocap=1}}. More at {{m|en|for-}}, {{m|en|own}}. Adjective from {{der|en|enm|-}}, passive participle of the verb {{m|enm|fraughten}}, from {{der|en|dum|vrachten}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[hire]] of a ship or boat to transport [[cargo]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Money paid to hire a ship or boat to transport cargo; [[freight]]
#: {{ux|en|'''fraught''' money}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[transportation]] of goods, especially in a ship or boat.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A ship's [[cargo]], [[lading]] or [[freight]].
#* '''1589''' or '''1590''', {{w|Christopher Marlowe}}, ''The Jew of Malta'', Act I, lines 129-31:
#*: Well, go,
#*: And bid the merchants and my men despatch,
#*: And come ashore, and see the '''fraught''' discharg'd.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1596|author={{w|William Shakespeare}} and {{w|Thomas Kyd}} ''(uncertain)''|title=[[w:Edward III (play)|Edward III]]|section=Act III, scene iv|url=https://archive.org/stream/cu31924013134337|passage={{...}} And now behold after my winters toyle, / My paynefull voyage on the boyſtrous ſea, / Of warres deuouring gulphes and ſteely rocks, / I bring my '''fraught''' vnto the wiſhed port / My Summers hope, my trauels ſweet reward {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} A [[load]]; a [[burden]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} Two [[bucketful]]s (of water).

